<section class="section section--cream">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:820px">
    <div class="text-center">
      <span class="section-label">How I approach forms</span>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-inline:auto">Every form has one job</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="principles-grid" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <div class="principle-card">
        <h3>Ask exactly enough</h3>
        <p>The more useful detail you have before the callback, the more jobs you close. I ask for the minimum, plus everything that helps you dispatch well.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle-card">
        <h3>Send each request to the right inbox</h3>
        <p>Emergency requests are flagged differently from routine ones. Commercial inquiries can go to their own address, and service-area forms can route by ZIP code.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle-card">
        <h3>Stop spam from the start</h3>
        <p>A spam filter and hidden honeypot fields catch bots, so customers never have to solve a puzzle (a CAPTCHA) to reach you. The form stays fast and clean.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle-card">
        <h3>Explain what happens next</h3>
        <p>Every form ends on a branded thank-you page that explains the next step, which cuts down on &ldquo;did it go through?&rdquo; calls.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle-card">
        <h3>Design for thumbs first</h3>
        <p>Most service-business forms are filled in on a phone. I design mine for thumbs first, then expand them for desktop.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle-card">
        <h3>Make every field accessible</h3>
        <p>Every input has a visible label, every error message is announced to screen readers, and every field meets WCAG 2.2 AA contrast.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

